---INSTRUCTION---
Write a function `max_palindrome_chars` that takes a string and returns the maximum number of characters that can be selected from the string (each character used at most as many times as it appears) to form a palindrome. Characters may be rearranged arbitrarily. A palindrome reads the same forward and backward, so at most one character may appear an odd number of times in the palindrome; all other characters must appear an even number of times.

The function should return an integer representing the length of the longest possible palindrome that can be constructed.

Examples:
- For input "aaabbbb", the longest palindrome uses 3 a's and 4 b's for a length of 7.
- For input "abccccdd", the longest palindrome uses 4 c's, 2 d's, and one of either a or b for a length of 7.
- For input "a", the longest palindrome uses the single character for a length of 1.

---TEST---
```elixir
defmodule SolutionTest do
  use ExUnit.Case, async: false

  test "example 1" do
    assert Solution.max_palindrome_chars("aaabbbb") == 7
  end

  test "example 2" do
    assert Solution.max_palindrome_chars("abccccdd") == 7
  end

  test "example 3" do
    assert Solution.max_palindrome_chars("a") == 1
  end
end
```

---REFERENCE---
```elixir
defmodule Solution do
  @spec max_palindrome_chars(String.t()) :: non_neg_integer()
  def max_palindrome_chars(s) do
    s
    |> String.graphemes()
    |> Enum.frequencies()
    |> Enum.reduce(0, fn {_char, count}, acc ->
      acc = acc + div(count, 2) * 2
      if rem(acc, 2) == 0 and rem(count, 2) == 1 do
        acc + 1
      else
        acc
      end
    end)
  end
end
```
